@database "image_compose"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: image_compose.guide 1.1 (14.10.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Image composition"
@{h1}Image composition

Lets an agent build a picture from layers it describes in JSON: a
background colour, images, text, rectangles, gradients, SVG graphics, a
vignette and drawings a script makes on a browser canvas, stacked
bottom-up and written as PNG, WEBP or JPEG. Every pixel
lands where the description says, so typography on a cover or an icon of
an exact size comes out the same every time. The canvas layer is the one
for anything drawn: icons, logos, illustrations, patterns, textures. Two measuring tools go with
it: one tells how bright and how busy a part of an image is, the other
finds the calmest place for a block of text.

The plugin also brings @{tt}image_agent@{utt}, a sub-agent that makes
game assets: it draws them on a canvas or generates them with ComfyUI,
composes with these tools and looks at the result.

The plugin has no panel and no hooks.

@{code}
For agents and developers
  @{"  Rendering: render  " link render}
  @{"  The layers  " link layers}
  @{"  Drawing with a script: canvas  " link canvas}
  @{"  Measuring: analyze, find_region  " link measure}
  @{"  Paths and limits  " link limits}
  @{"  The image agent  " link agent}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node render "Rendering: render"
@{h1}Rendering

The tool is the instance name followed by @{tt}_render@{utt}
(@{tt}image_compose_render@{utt}, @{tt}images_render@{utt} for the image
agent's instance). It renders one spec to one file.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
spec | the composition: an object, or the same as a JSON string (required)
output_path | the file to write; project-relative or absolute (required)
include_content | attach the rendered image to the answer, for the model to look at; default true
layers_dir | where each layer is also saved as its own PNG; omitted: <output name>_layers next to the file; "" or false: no export
spec_path | also save the spec as JSON (UTF-8, umlauts kept as they are) to this path
@{body}

@{h2}The spec

@{table}
Key | Meaning
size | [width, height] in pixels, whole numbers 1-8192; default [1024, 1536]
background | a colour, or "transparent" (default)
format | png, webp or jpeg (jpg is taken); default: from the file extension, else png
quality | for webp and jpeg, default 95
layers | the layers, drawn first to last (@{" The layers " link layers})
@{body}

JPEG has no transparency: the picture is laid onto white first. WEBP is
written with the slowest, best compression; PNG optimized. An existing
file is overwritten. Missing folders on the way to the file are created.

@{h2}The layer export

Unless switched off, every layer is also saved as a transparent PNG the
size of the canvas, @{tt}layer_00_gradient.png@{utt},
@{tt}layer_01_text.png@{utt} and so on, with the layer where it sits in
the composite -- for editing one layer later or reusing it. Before that,
every @{tt}layer_*.png@{utt} already in the folder is deleted, so the
folder shows this composition and no layer from an earlier one. For that
reason @{tt}layers_dir@{utt} may not be the folder the picture itself goes
to; that is refused before anything is written.

@{h2}The answer

@{table}
Field | Meaning
status | success, or warning when there are warnings -- the file is written either way
output_path | the absolute path written
size, format, bytes | of the file
layers_rendered | how many layers were drawn
warnings | what looks wrong (below); at most 20, then "... and N more warnings"
layer_files | the exported layer PNGs
spec_path | when the spec was saved
action_required | with warnings: an instruction to fix them and render again
@{body}

A failed render answers @{tt}status: error@{utt} with @{tt}error@{utt}
and @{tt}error_type@{utt} (@{tt}ValidationError@{utt} for a missing spec
or output path, @{tt}CompositionError@{utt} for the rest). A layer's
error names it: "layer 2 (text): text align must be left|center|right,
got 'middle'". Nothing is written then, except folders created on the way.

@{h2}Warnings

@{bullet}A layer that reaches past the canvas edge, with the overhang per
side. The vignette is never checked; everything else is, also a picture
meant to bleed off the edge.
@{bullet}Two text or SVG layers that overlap, or sit closer than
@{tt}overlap_min_gap_px@{utt} (default 30) side by side or one above the
other. Layers that are apart on both axes -- diagonal neighbours -- are
fine. Other layer types are not compared: text over a picture is the
normal case. Switched off with @{tt}overlap_check_enabled: false@{utt}.
@{bullet}A font that was not found (the text is drawn with a fallback).
@{bullet}A value that was repaired: a layer type with trailing words
("text, bold" becomes text), a text size that is not a number, a stroke
or shadow that is not an object.

@{h2}Example

@{code json}
{"output_path": "data/workspace/images/title.png",
 "spec": {"size": [1024, 512], "background": "#101820",
   "layers": [
     {"type": "gradient", "colors": ["#10182000", "#f2aa4c"], "direction": "bottom"},
     {"type": "text", "text": "Northern Lights", "font": "serif_bold", "size": 96,
      "color": "#ffffff", "align": "center",
      "position": {"anchor": "center", "offset": [0, -20]},
      "shadow": {"offset": [3, 3], "blur": 6, "color": "#00000099"}}]}}
@{body}
@endnode

@node layers "The layers"
@{h1}The layers

Every layer is an object with a @{tt}type@{utt}: image, text, rect,
gradient, svg, vignette or canvas (@{" Drawing with a script " link canvas}).

@{h2}What every layer takes

@{table}
Key | Meaning
position | where its top-left corner goes: [x, y] in pixels; or {"anchor": a, "offset": [dx, dy]}; or {"anchor": a, "offset_pct": [dx %, dy %]} of the canvas; or just the anchor name. Default [0, 0]
opacity | 0 to 1, default 1; outside that range is an error
rotation | degrees, clockwise, around the layer's centre; the layer grows to hold the turned picture
blend_mode | normal (default), multiply, screen or overlay
@{body}

The nine anchors: top_left, top_center, top_right, center_left, center,
center_right, bottom_left, bottom_center, bottom_right. An anchor puts the
matching point of the layer on the matching point of the canvas --
bottom_right puts the layer's lower right corner in the canvas's -- and
the offset moves it from there.

Colours are @{tt}#rgb@{utt}, @{tt}#rgba@{utt}, @{tt}#rrggbb@{utt},
@{tt}#rrggbbaa@{utt} or [r, g, b] / [r, g, b, a].

@{h2}image

@{table}
Key | Meaning
src | a file (project-relative or absolute) or a data: URI with base64 (required)
size | [w, h], or {"width": w, "height": h}, or {"width_pct": %, "height_pct": %} of the canvas; without it the picture keeps its own size
fit | with a size: cover (default; fills and crops the middle), contain (fits whole, transparent margins) or stretch; any other word acts as cover
cutout | true: removes the background with rembg's isnet-general-use model before the fit; or a model name, e.g. birefnet-general-lite (keeps thin parts, about five times slower), isnet-anime, u2net
trim | true: crops to the visible part before the fit, so an object fills its size instead of the empty frame around it
alpha_threshold | 1-255: after the fit, every pixel becomes fully opaque (alpha at or above it) or fully clear -- for pixel art
@{body}

A size needs both sides; one alone is an error. "true"/"false" as
strings work for cutout and trim. An alpha_threshold of 0 or true is
refused -- leave the key out instead. The rembg model is downloaded once
per computer into @{tt}~/.rembg@{utt} on first use (isnet about 170 MB).

@{h2}text

@{table}
Key | Meaning
text | the text (required); line breaks as \n
font | an alias (sans, sans_bold, serif, serif_bold, mono, display, display_grotesk, script, stencil, display_block), a file in the fonts folder, an absolute path, or a system font name; default sans
size | font size in pixels, default 48, at least 4
color | default white
align | left (default), center or right, within the block
max_width | wrapping width in pixels or {"width_pct": %}; default the canvas width less 160
line_height | factor, default 1.1
tracking | extra pixels between letters, default 0
uppercase | true: capitals
stroke | {"width": px, "color": default black}
shadow | {"offset": [dx, dy], "blur": px, "color": default #00000088}
@{body}

Words wrap at spaces; a word too long for a line is broken between
letters. The layer is the text block plus a margin on each side for
stroke and shadow (the larger of the stroke width and blur plus offset,
plus 4 pixels), and position places that box.

Where a font comes from, in order: an absolute path; a file of that name
in the fonts folder; @{tt}font_aliases@{utt}; a list of common system
fonts for each alias (Georgia, Arial, Consolas, Impact, DejaVu ...); the
name as a system font; Pillow's small built-in font, with a warning.

@{h2}rect

@{table}
Key | Meaning
rect | [x, y, w, h]; or position with size as for image; neither: the whole canvas
fill | colour; none: only the border
radius | rounded corners, pixels
border | {"width": px, "color": default black}
@{body}

@{h2}gradient

@{table}
Key | Meaning
rect | or position with size, as for rect
colors | two or more colours ("from" and "to" work too)
stops | where each colour sits, 0 to 1; default evenly spaced
direction | where the gradient ends: right, bottom_right, bottom (default), bottom_left, left, top_left, top, top_right, or an angle in degrees (0 right, 90 down)
@{body}

The first colour is on the side opposite the direction, the last on the
direction's side. A colour with alpha fades a picture beneath.

@{h2}svg

@{table}
Key | Meaning
svg | the SVG as text (required)
size | [w, h]: scaled to fit inside, keeping its proportions; without it, the SVG's own size
@{body}

Rendered with svglib and reportlab, twice -- on black and on white -- to
recover real transparency, so white parts stay white. The SVG cannot load
files; PNG and JPEG images embedded as data: URIs work. A font-family is
looked up by name among the installed fonts; a family that looks like a
path (with /, \\ or :) is drawn in Helvetica instead of being opened.
Drawing needs reportlab's cairo backend, an optional part of the install
(see Dependencies under @{" Setting it up " link setup}); without it an
svg layer fails with a message that names the fix.

@{h2}vignette

Darkens towards the corners over the whole canvas; position is ignored.
@{tt}strength@{utt} 0 to 1 (default 0.3), @{tt}color@{utt} (default
black). The centre area, up to 40 % of the way to the corners, stays
clear.
@endnode

@node canvas "Drawing with a script: canvas"
@{h1}Drawing with a script

A canvas layer is drawn by JavaScript: the script runs in a headless
Chromium against the canvas 2D API a model knows from the web -- paths,
@{tt}Path2D@{utt} (also from SVG path strings), gradients, shadows,
@{tt}ctx.filter@{utt}, patterns, text, @{tt}getImageData@{utt}. Models
draw far better this way than by stacking rect and gradient layers, and
the result is a layer like any other: it takes position, opacity,
rotation and blend mode, and other layers go above and below it.

@{table}
Key | Meaning
script | the drawing, a function body (required); in scope: ctx, w, h, img
size | [w, h] of the layer's canvas, at most 8192 per side; default: the whole canvas
seed | a number, default 1 (anything else counts as 1); Math.random and the noise are seeded with it
images | {name: source} -- each is img.name in the script, ready for ctx.drawImage
fonts | [alias or file] -- each is a font family in ctx.font, named in quotes: '40px "display"'
@{body}

@{h2}The script

@{bullet}It is the body of an async function: @{tt}ctx@{utt} is the 2D
context, @{tt}w@{utt} and @{tt}h@{utt} the layer's size in pixels, one
canvas pixel is one picture pixel. @{tt}await@{utt} works; waiting costs
no real time. The script may declare @{tt}w@{utt}, @{tt}h@{utt} or
@{tt}ctx@{utt} again, as a model does by habit. A script that changes the
canvas' size is refused: the size is the layer's.
@{bullet}What it does not paint stays transparent.
@{bullet}@{tt}Math.random@{utt} is seeded: the same script with the same
seed is the same picture, another seed is a variation of it.
@{bullet}An image source is a path (as for an image layer), a data: URI
of an image, or SVG markup -- the browser rasterises an SVG at the size
it is drawn, with everything SVG can do. A file named .svg has to be
SVG.
@{bullet}A font is a configured alias, one of the text layer's built-in
names (display, serif_bold, ...: the same system font), a file of the
fonts folder or an absolute path, loaded before the script runs. Its
family in the script is the alias, or the file's name ("Oswald-Bold.ttf",
for an absolute path too) -- in quotes, since a name with a dot is no
family without them and the browser then ignores the whole
@{tt}ctx.font@{utt} line. It must be a font file -- anything else is
refused, since the script could read it back out of the page. A font the
system has needs no entry; name its family.
@{bullet}An error message is cut at 500 characters.
@{bullet}An error answers with the script's own line: "layer 0 (canvas):
canvas layer: line 12: nope is not defined". Nothing is written then.

@{h2}What the page adds

@{table}
Function | Meaning
noise(x, y, period) | smooth noise from 0 to 1 on a unit grid; with a period it repeats every that many units
fbm(x, y, octaves, period) | layered noise (default 4 octaves), same range, same rule
pixels(ctx, (x, y) => [r, g, b, a]) | sets every pixel from the function, 0 to 255; a is optional
tile(ctx, () => { ... }) | draws at the nine offsets of a tiling, each with the same random numbers
@{body}

For a seamless texture, sample the noise so that the layer is a whole
number of periods wide: @{tt}noise(x / w * 8, y / h * 8, 8)@{utt}. Shapes
become seamless through @{tt}tile@{utt}: whatever crosses an edge comes
back in on the opposite one. Fill the background outside @{tt}tile@{utt}
and wrap each shape (or group) in its own call -- a background painted
inside would cover the copies drawn before it.

@{code json}
{"type": "canvas", "size": [512, 512], "seed": 11, "script":
 "pixels(ctx, (x, y) => { const v = 60 + fbm(x / w * 8, y / h * 8, 4, 8) * 40; return [v, v, v]; });\nfor (let i = 0; i < 40; i++) { const x = Math.random() * w, y = Math.random() * h, r = 20 + Math.random() * 30;\n  tile(ctx, () => { ctx.fillStyle = `hsl(30 20% ${40 + Math.random() * 30}%)`; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(x, y, r, 0, 7); ctx.fill(); }); }"}
@{body}

@{h2}What the page cannot do

It has no network, reads no files and saves none. Three walls, each
with its own test:

@{bullet}The page is served from a loopback address of the plugin's own
process, not opened as a file. A file page may navigate to any file URL
-- on Windows also to file://host/share, which signs in to that host --
an http page may not.
@{bullet}The browser is started with a proxy nobody listens on, for
every address but the page's own. No request of the page or of the
browser itself leaves the machine: not a fetch, not a navigation, not a
form.
@{bullet}A Content-Security-Policy allows only what is inside the page
(images and fonts travel into it as data: URIs) -- no name is looked up
either -- and puts it in a sandbox that may run scripts and nothing
else: no download -- without it a script saved files into the user's
Downloads folder -- no form, no window.

A page that navigates away has no picture to give: "the browser returned
no picture". One gap is known: WebRTC can send UDP past a proxy. In
eleven runs without the proxy one such packet reached a local listener,
in eleven behind it none -- too rare to test, so it is not claimed
closed. What a script could send that way is what it was given: its own
text and the images and fonts the layer names. And the proxy is a
command-line flag: a machine policy that sets the browser's proxy
(ProxyMode) overrides it.

A script runs for at most @{tt}canvas_timeout@{utt} seconds (default
60); then the browser is killed and the layer fails with "the script ran
longer than 60s (an endless loop?)". Its child processes end with it,
the renderer in the endless loop included -- tested with Edge on Windows
and with Debian's Chromium as root (--no-sandbox) in a container
(tests/linux.Dockerfile builds it and runs the canvas tests). A
container whose first process is the plugin's own Python keeps them as
zombies, dead but unreaped; start it with an init (docker run --init).
Each canvas
layer starts a browser of its own -- about a second, a 4096 x 4096 layer
about five; one of random pixels takes 19 seconds and 300 MB in the
plugin's process.

Needs a Chromium on the server (@{" Setting it up " link setup}).
@endnode

@node measure "Measuring: analyze, find_region"
@{h1}Measuring

So an agent can choose a text colour and a place for the text from the
pixels rather than from a guess.

@{h2}analyze

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
path | the image, project-relative or absolute (required)
region | [x, y, w, h] in pixels; clipped to the image; default the whole image
@{body}

@{table}
Field | Meaning
region, size | the area measured, after clipping
brightness | mean luminance (Rec. 709), 0 dark to 1 light
brightness_std | its spread; above about 0.2 the area is uneven
brightness_min, brightness_max, brightness_range | darkest and lightest of 4x4 cells of the area
edge_density | how much fine detail; above about 0.1 is busy
homogeneity | 1 perfectly calm, 0 busy -- the worse of spread and detail
dominant_color, palette | the most common colours, rounded to steps of 32, with their share (top five)
recommendation | light_text (brightness under 0.4), dark_text (over 0.6), midtone_uncertain_use_backdrop, or mixed_use_stroke_or_pill when some cells are under 0.4 and others over 0.6 and they differ by more than 0.3
@{body}

@{h2}find_region

Slides a window of the given size over the image and ranks the places by
homogeneity.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
path | the image (required)
region_size | [w, h] of the text block (required); must fit in the image
prefer | any (default), top_third, bottom_third, top_half or bottom_half: the band the window starts in
max_candidates | how many places to return; default 3, held to 1-10
@{body}

The window moves in steps of a sixteenth of the free room, so at most
17 x 17 places are tried. With top_third or top_half the window starts in
that band but may reach below it when it is taller than the band.

The answer: @{tt}image_size@{utt}, @{tt}search_size@{utt},
@{tt}prefer@{utt}, @{tt}scanned@{utt} (places tried), @{tt}best@{utt} and
@{tt}candidates@{utt}. Each place has @{tt}region@{utt} and the same
figures as analyze (without palette) and a recommendation.

@{code json}
{"path": "data/workspace/images/background.png", "region_size": [800, 200], "prefer": "bottom_third"}
@{body}

Both answer errors as @{tt}status: error@{utt}: a missing file is a
@{tt}FileNotFoundError@{utt}, a malformed region, region_size or max_candidates
a @{tt}ValidationError@{utt}.
@endnode

@node limits "Paths and limits"
@{h1}Paths and limits

@{h2}Paths

@{bullet}A relative path counts from the folder the server started in (the
project). A @{tt}data/...@{utt} path lands in the data directory, also
when that has been moved elsewhere.
@{bullet}A network or device path (@{tt}\\\\host\\share\\x.png@{utt},
@{tt}//host/share/x.png@{utt}) is refused on its text, before anything is
opened -- touching it would make Windows sign in to that host with your
credentials. This holds for every path from the model: output_path,
layers_dir, spec_path, an image layer's src, a text layer's font, and the
images the measuring tools read. A font-family inside an SVG that looks like
a path is not opened at all. A share that is one of the
@{tt}output_directories@{utt} may be written.
@{bullet}Reading is not confined: any file on the computer the server can
read can be used as an image layer or measured.

@{h2}The write sandbox

With @{tt}output_directories@{utt} empty (the default) an instance writes
wherever it is told. With a list, the picture, its layer folder and the
spec file must all lie inside one of those folders; anything else is
refused before anything is created: "output_path <path> lies outside the
allowed output directories (<folders>)". Give every instance a sub-agent
drives a folder of its own this way, as the image agent's
@{tt}images@{utt} instance does.

@{h2}Size limits

@{bullet}The canvas is at most 8192 x 8192.
@{bullet}No picture any step holds may have more pixels than that: an image
file or data URI (checked from its header, before it is decoded), a
layer's size (a canvas layer's too), an image a canvas layer loads, a
text block, an SVG's drawing size, a PNG or JPEG embedded in
an SVG (checked from its header too), and the intermediate
picture a cover fit scales to. Anything larger is refused: "layer 0
(rect): layer 100000x100000 is too large (max 67108864 pixels)".
@{bullet}There is no limit on the number of layers. Memory grows with them:
every layer is held until the file is written, and with the layer export
on, each is saved as a PNG the size of the canvas.
@{bullet}Gradients and vignettes are computed pixel by pixel in Python: a
full 1024 x 1536 canvas takes a second or two, a full 8192 x 8192 one
close to a minute (measured: 4096 x 4096 in 8-12 seconds).
@{bullet}A canvas script is at most 200,000 characters and runs for at
most @{tt}canvas_timeout@{utt} seconds; what its page prints back is
capped at 512 MB, more than any picture needs.
@{bullet}There is no other timeout; a cancelled call returns, the render in
its thread runs to the end.

@{h2}What is left behind

Nothing is cleaned up: the pictures, the layer folders and saved specs
stay. The render is not recorded by
@{" file checkpoints " link file_checkpoints/recorded}: a rewind counts it
but cannot restore a file it overwrote.
@endnode

@node agent "The image agent"
@{h1}The image agent

@{tt}image_agent@{utt} produces one texture, sprite, icon or UI element
as a PNG of an exact size. It is called as a tool
(@{tt}image_agent_execute_task@{utt}) or started as a sub-agent -- the
@{tt}gamedev@{utt} agent of the godot plugin lists it in its sub-agent
manager. It runs in a session of its own, so the pictures it looks at
stay out of its caller's context.

Its loop, from its prompt and the always-loaded skill
@{tt}image-assets@{utt}:

@{bullet}Read the brief for five facts: what, exact size, tileable or not,
background, style; fill a missing one with a plain default and say so.
@{bullet}What can be drawn is drawn, with a canvas layer: icons, UI
elements, logos, flat illustrations, patterns, procedural textures, pixel
art. Anything photographic or painterly is generated with ComfyUI first.
@{bullet}Look at every generated picture and regenerate on a miss, at most
three times.
@{bullet}Compose onto a canvas of the exact size; sprites get their
transparency from @{tt}cutout@{utt}, @{tt}trim@{utt} and, for pixel art,
@{tt}alpha_threshold@{utt}.
@{bullet}A tileable texture is rendered 2 x 2 and the seams looked at.
@{bullet}It reports a five-line block: Path (as the render returned it),
Size, Tileable, Background, Verified.

Its tools: @{tt}comfyui/*@{utt}, @{tt}images/*@{utt} (its own
@{tt}image_compose@{utt} instance, confined to
@{tt}data/workspace/images@{utt}), @{tt}media_ops/*@{utt} (to look at a
file), @{tt}skills/*@{utt}, @{tt}context_engineer/*@{utt} and
@{tt}datetime/*@{utt}. The context engineer hook is on for it, since every
picture it looks at stays in its context. Models: Gemini Flash, Claude
Sonnet as fallback (profiles @{tt}or-gemini-flash@{utt},
@{tt}or-claude-sonnet@{utt}); at most 60 steps.

Both the agent and the @{tt}images@{utt} instance are defined in
@{tt}agents/image_agent.yaml@{utt} in the plugin folder and are loaded
with the plugin; it needs a running ComfyUI (see
@{" ComfyUI " link comfyui/main}) for anything it generates.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

Three tools, described in the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. The
render tool's spec parameter carries a compact reference of all layer
fields, so it is the longest description and is sent with every request
that offers the tool. Limits and defaults are not all in it; the model
learns them from refusals. Texts the model gets include:

@{code}
spec is not valid JSON: <parser message>
layer 2 (text): text align must be left|center|right, got 'middle'
layer 0 (image): image src not found: <path>
layer 1 (gradient): unknown gradient direction: 'down'
output_path <path> lies outside the allowed output directories (<folders>)
output_path \\\\host\\share\\x.png is a network or device path
layers_dir <path> is the directory the composite is written to; the per-layer export needs one of its own (omit layers_dir for <name>_layers, or pass "" to skip it)
region_size (900, 200) must fit inside image 800x600
max_candidates must be an integer 1-10
layer 0 (canvas): canvas layer: line 12: nope is not defined
layer 0 (canvas): canvas layer: the script ran longer than 60s (an endless loop?)
layer 0 (canvas): canvas layer: the script resized the canvas to 2048x2048; the layer is 512x512 -- set `size` on the layer instead
layer 0 (canvas): canvas layer: font 'notes.txt' (notes.txt) is not a font file
layer 0 (canvas): canvas layer: images.wood not found: <path>
layer 0 (canvas): canvas layer: no Chromium browser found for canvas layers (Edge, Chrome or Chromium): install one, or set `browser` in this server's config to its path
layer 0 (svg): SVG layers need reportlab's cairo backend (rlPyCairo), which is not installed on this server. To add it: install cairo, pkg-config, a C compiler and Python's headers (macOS: brew install cairo pkg-config; Debian/Ubuntu: sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y build-essential libcairo2-dev pkg-config [python<3.X>-dev, where Python.h is missing]; Windows needs none of them), then pip install -r requirements/optional.txt in the server's virtual environment, or run install.sh (Windows: install.ps1) again. Every other layer type works without it. (import rlPyCairo: <reason>)
@{body}

A render with warnings answers @{tt}status: warning@{utt} and adds
@{tt}action_required@{utt}: "Re-compose: address each item in `warnings`
(e.g. reduce layer size, change position/anchor, shorten title) and call
image_compose_render again. Don't accept the cover as-is when warnings are
present." -- with the plugin's own tool name, not the instance's.

The chat's status line ends with "Rendered title.png: 1024x512 (48213
bytes) -- 1 warning(s)", "brightness=0.23 std=0.08 -> light_text" or
"best region x=112,y=1180,w=800,h=200 homogeneity=0.91 -> light_text".

@{h2}The attached picture

With @{tt}include_content@{utt} (the default) the rendered file is
attached to the answer. The conversation keeps its path; each request
reads the file again and sends it along, so it must stay where it is and
a file rendered again under the same name is seen as it is now. A model
without image input gets a note instead, as for
@{" media_ops " link media_ops/model}.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Answers are appended; nothing earlier changes. An answer is a few hundred
tokens, more with warnings. An attached picture costs what the provider
charges -- the core's context estimate counts 1,120 tokens per image -- on
every later request of the run. A model that renders, looks and renders
again carries every version: pass @{tt}include_content: false@{utt} for
intermediate renders, or let the context engineer evict old pictures. The
attachment is built the same way each time and does not break the prompt
cache.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    image_compose:
      type: image_compose
      enabled: true
      description: "Compose layered images (background + text + shapes + gradients + SVG)"
      fonts_dir: "src/plugins/image_compose/fonts"   # plugin-bundled assets
      overlap_check_enabled: true   # warn on text/svg BBox overlap pairs after render
      overlap_min_gap_px: 30        # required clearance between text/svg layers (px)
@{body}

An agent sees the tools when its allowlist names them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+image_compose/*"
@{body}

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
fonts_dir | the data directory's fonts folder | where font files and aliases are looked up; created at start
font_aliases | none | alias name to a file in fonts_dir, or an absolute path
output_directories | [] (write anywhere) | the write sandbox (@{" Paths and limits " link limits})
overlap_check_enabled | true | the overlap warnings
overlap_min_gap_px | 30 | the gap two text or SVG layers must keep
browser | looked for | the Chromium that draws canvas layers: a path, or a name on PATH
browser_args | [] | extra flags for it; a container running as root needs ["--no-sandbox"]
canvas_timeout | 60 | seconds a canvas script may run
@{body}

The keys are read from the entry itself when the server starts. A
relative folder counts from the folder the server started in.

@{h2}Fonts

The plugin's @{tt}fonts/@{utt} folder holds a README with the intended
set -- Inter, Crimson Pro, JetBrains Mono, Bebas Neue, Oswald, Caveat,
Stardos Stencil, Bungee Inline, all from Google Fonts -- but no font
files. Until you add them and map them, every alias falls back to the
system fonts:

@{code yaml}
      font_aliases:
        display_grotesk: "Oswald-Bold.ttf"
        script: "Caveat-Regular.ttf"
@{body}

@{h2}The browser for canvas layers

Canvas layers need a Chromium on the server: Edge (part of Windows),
Chrome or Chromium. Without @{tt}browser@{utt} it is looked for where
Windows and macOS put it, then as @{tt}chromium@{utt},
@{tt}chromium-browser@{utt}, @{tt}google-chrome@{utt} or
@{tt}microsoft-edge@{utt} on PATH, then where Playwright downloads one.
On Debian or Ubuntu: @{tt}sudo apt-get install -y chromium@{utt}. A
server that runs as root (a container) also needs
@{tt}browser_args: ["--no-sandbox"]@{utt}. Without a browser the plugin
logs a WARNING at start and every canvas layer answers with that fix; all
other layers work. For the length of a render the plugin listens on a
loopback port of its own choosing and serves the page there, once, at an
address with a random token. The token is on the browser's command line:
where other users of the machine can read that (Linux without hidepid),
one of them can fetch the page -- the script, its images and fonts --
before the browser does; the render then fails.

@{h2}Dependencies

Pillow, and with the plugin: numpy (measuring and SVG), svglib with
reportlab (SVG layers) and @{tt}rembg[cpu]@{utt} (cutout). Each is
imported only when a layer needs it; without it, that layer fails with a
message naming the package.

SVG layers are drawn through reportlab's cairo backend,
@{tt}reportlab[pycairo]@{utt}. It is an optional dependency
(@{tt}requirements/optional.txt@{utt}, not part of
@{tt}pip install -e .@{utt}): pycairo has wheels for Windows only and is
built everywhere else, against cairo and pkg-config. The install scripts
add it -- install.sh installs cairo first where it can (Homebrew; apt-get
with sudo) -- and go on without it when that fails. Without it the plugin
logs a WARNING at start and every svg layer answers with the fix: cairo,
pkg-config, a compiler and Python's headers (macOS
@{tt}brew install cairo pkg-config@{utt}, Debian/Ubuntu
@{tt}sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y build-essential
libcairo2-dev pkg-config@{utt}, plus @{tt}python3.X-dev@{utt} where the
server's Python 3.X lacks its headers),
then
@{tt}pip install -r requirements/optional.txt@{utt} or the install script
again. The Docker image carries it.
@endnode
